# ============================================================
# Vision — Docker Compose environment  (reference template)
# ============================================================
# NOTE: You normally don't need to touch this.
# The Vision desktop app (/Applications/Vision.app) creates a real .env
# automatically on first launch with a securely generated random password.
#
# Only use this file if you are running docker compose manually
# without the desktop app:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   # replace the placeholder values with a real password:
#   openssl rand -hex 32   # paste into both fields below
#
# .env is git-ignored; never commit the real file.
# ============================================================

# --- Database (required) ---
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=replace-with-output-of-openssl-rand-hex-32
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://ftm_user:replace-with-output-of-openssl-rand-hex-32@db:5432/financial_transactions

# --- Least-privilege DB role (optional hardening) ---
# By default the backend's runtime pool connects as ftm_user — the Postgres
# bootstrap superuser — so any SQL injection or compromised dependency has
# instance-level reach. To run the app's runtime pool on a non-superuser role,
# set these TWO (the desktop app writes exactly this layout for you):
#
#   1. POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD  — a second `openssl rand -hex 32` value
#   2. DATABASE_URL_APP       — the same URL as DATABASE_URL but for ftm_app
#
# DATABASE_URL itself STAYS on ftm_user. It is the privileged/DDL connection
# (Alembic, the role bootstrap, the desktop backup/restore tooling), and it is
# also the only one an app image predating this feature understands — leaving
# it alone is what lets the desktop shell and the backend image update
# independently without a boot failure.
#
# This works on EXISTING databases, not only fresh ones. Two mechanisms create
# the role, and they agree with each other:
#   - docker/postgres-init/01-app-role.sh, on a data volume being initialised
#     for the very first time (it reads POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD);
#   - the backend's boot-time bootstrap
#     (apps/node-backend/src/database/appRoleBootstrap.js), on every boot,
#     which creates/repairs the role and its grants over DATABASE_URL and takes
#     the password from DATABASE_URL_APP.
#
# The bootstrap FAILS OPEN: if anything about it does not verify, the runtime
# pool simply stays on DATABASE_URL (i.e. the previous single-role behaviour)
# and the reason is logged at error level — enabling this can never stop the
# app from booting.
#
# POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD=second-output-of-openssl-rand-hex-32
# DATABASE_URL_APP=postgresql://ftm_app:second-output-of-openssl-rand-hex-32@db:5432/financial_transactions
#
# DATABASE_URL_MIGRATIONS overrides which URL is treated as privileged. It is
# only needed for the older hand-rolled layout where DATABASE_URL itself was
# repointed at ftm_app; alembic/env.py prefers it when set.
# DATABASE_URL_MIGRATIONS=postgresql://ftm_user:replace-with-output-of-openssl-rand-hex-32@db:5432/financial_transactions
#
# Escape hatch: set VISION_DISABLE_APP_ROLE_BOOTSTRAP=true to keep the runtime
# pool on DATABASE_URL without removing DATABASE_URL_APP.
# VISION_DISABLE_APP_ROLE_BOOTSTRAP=true

# --- Logging (optional, production defaults shown) ---
LOG_LEVEL=warn
ENABLE_LOGGING=true

# --- Research providers (ADR-079, all optional) — SHARED SECRETS ---
# Single home for the research provider API keys (ADR-080): this root .env is read
# by Docker (env_file) AND by local dev (loadDotenv layers it under the backend
# .env.local). Set them here once. Each is OPTIONAL: a provider with no key is
# dropped from the research capability chain (Yahoo needs no key).
# Uncomment and fill in the ones you want to enable.
# TWELVE_DATA_API_KEY=        # quotes/charts        — free tier 8/min, 800/day
# FINNHUB_API_KEY=            # news, US fundamentals — free tier 60/min
# FMP_API_KEY=                # fundamentals         — free tier 250/day
# ALPHA_VANTAGE_API_KEY=      # fallback quotes/fund. — free tier ~25/day
# FRED_API_KEY=               # macro series (ADR-082) — free tier (Eurostat/DBnomics need no key)
